import type { QuestionSeed } from "./types";

/**
 * Hard Questions about evil, Satan and judgement. Original FCGBS text. Positions held by different Christian traditions are
 * stated fairly; historical and archaeological statements are limited to well-established evidence.
 */
export const EVIL_QUESTIONS: QuestionSeed[] = [
  { slug: "why-does-god-allow-evil-and-suffering", category: "God, evil and judgement", featured: true,
    title: "Why does God allow evil and suffering?",
    summary: "The Bible does not give a single tidy explanation, but it gives several truths that belong together: God made a good world and is not the author of evil; evil entered through the free rebellion of creatures; God is sovereign and can bring good even from evil; He entered suffering in Christ and defeated evil on the cross; and He promises to end it completely. Meanwhile He invites us to trust Him, to lament honestly and to care for the suffering.",
    scriptures: ["Genesis 1:31", "Genesis 3:1-19", "Genesis 50:15-21", "Job 38:1-41", "Psalms 73:1-28", "Habakkuk 1:1-17", "John 9:1-3", "Luke 13:1-5", "Romans 8:18-28", "2 Corinthians 4:16-18", "Revelation 21:1-5"],
    readings: ["C. S. Lewis, The Problem of Pain", "Alvin Plantinga, God, Freedom, and Evil", "D. A. Carson, How Long, O Lord?", "John Stott, The Cross of Christ (chapter 12)", "N. T. Wright, Evil and the Justice of God"],
    exegetical: `## Where the Bible begins
"And God saw every thing that he had made, and, behold, it was very good" (Genesis 1:31, KJV). Evil is not part of the original creation. Genesis 3 shows how it entered: the serpent questioned God's word and the man and woman chose to disobey. Death, pain, toil and broken relationships follow (Genesis 3:14–19). Paul says that "by one man sin entered into the world, and death by sin" (Romans 5:12, KJV).

## God is not the author of sin
"Let no man say when he is tempted, I am tempted of God: for God cannot be tempted with evil, neither tempteth he any man: But every man is tempted, when he is drawn away of his own lust, and enticed" (James 1:13–14, KJV). Habakkuk says of God, "Thou art of purer eyes than to behold evil, and canst not look on iniquity" (Habakkuk 1:13, KJV).

## But God is not absent
Joseph told his brothers who had sold him: "But as for you, ye thought evil against me; but God meant it unto good, to bring to pass, as it is this day, to save much people alive" (Genesis 50:20, KJV). The same event is both a human crime and part of God's wise purpose. The Bible never excuses the evil act, and it never says that God lost control.

## Job: the honest questioner
Job suffers terribly, and his friends insist that he must have sinned. The book rejects that explanation (God says they have not spoken what is right, Job 42:7). When God finally speaks (chapters 38–41) He does not explain the reason for Job's suffering; He shows His wisdom and power in creation and invites trust. Job's final response: "I know that thou canst do every thing, and that no thought can be withholden from thee" (Job 42:2, KJV). The prologue (chapters 1–2) shows a dimension of conflict that Job did not see.

## Jesus on suffering
- **Not always punishment.** Asked about a man born blind, Jesus said, "Neither hath this man sinned, nor his parents: but that the works of God should be made manifest in him" (John 9:3, KJV).
- **Disasters are warnings, not scoreboards.** When Pilate killed Galileans and a tower fell on eighteen people, Jesus said these were not worse sinners than others, and added, "except ye repent, ye shall all likewise perish" (Luke 13:1–5).
- **He wept.** At Lazarus's tomb "Jesus wept" (John 11:35).

## Creation groaning
"For we know that the whole creation groaneth and travaileth in pain together until now" (Romans 8:22, KJV). The created order is "subject to vanity" and will be set free (8:20–21). This helps explain natural evil: earthquakes, disease and death belong to a creation under the curse, awaiting renewal.`,
    archaeological: `## An ancient question
The problem of suffering is as old as civilisation, and the Bible's honesty about it stands alongside other ancient texts:
- **"The Babylonian Theodicy"** (about 1000 BC) is a dialogue between a sufferer and a friend who defends the gods; the gods are said to have made humanity prone to lies and injustice.
- **"I will praise the Lord of wisdom" (*Ludlul bēl nēmeqi*)**, a Babylonian poem about a righteous man who suffers, is compared with Job.
- **The Sumerian "Man and His God"** is an even older example of the plea of an innocent sufferer.
These texts show that the problem of undeserved suffering was explored in Mesopotamia before and during Israel's history. What differs in the Bible: one good God, creator of all, the only source of power; evil as a rebellion, not part of the divine order; a God who enters suffering Himself.

## Evidence of human cruelty and hope
Excavated destruction layers (for example the burning of Lachish by Assyria in 701 BC and by Babylon in 588 BC), the Lachish reliefs in Nineveh depicting deportations, and the Lachish letters written in the last days of Judah's kingdom illustrate the human reality behind the prophets' warnings and laments (Lamentations, Habakkuk, Jeremiah).`,
    apostolic: `The apostles do not deny suffering; they put it in the light of the cross and the coming glory.
- **Paul:** "For I reckon that the sufferings of this present time are not worthy to be compared with the glory which shall be revealed in us" (Romans 8:18, KJV). "And we know that all things work together for good to them that love God, to them who are the called according to his purpose" (Romans 8:28, KJV) — a promise to believers, not a claim that every event is good.
- **Paul on his own suffering:** "For our light affliction, which is but for a moment, worketh for us a far more exceeding and eternal weight of glory" (2 Corinthians 4:17, KJV). He also prayed three times about his thorn (12:7–10).
- **Peter:** "Beloved, think it not strange concerning the fiery trial which is to try you, as though some strange thing happened unto you" (1 Peter 4:12, KJV).
- **James:** trials produce endurance (James 1:2–4).
- **John:** "God is light, and in him is no darkness at all" (1 John 1:5, KJV).`,
    prophetic: `## The promise that evil will end
- **The first promise:** the seed of the woman will crush the serpent's head (Genesis 3:15).
- **The suffering servant:** "Surely he hath borne our griefs, and carried our sorrows... and with his stripes we are healed" (Isaiah 53:4–5, KJV).
- **A new creation:** "For, behold, I create new heavens and a new earth" (Isaiah 65:17, KJV), where the wolf and lamb feed together (65:25).
- **The final word:** "And God shall wipe away all tears from their eyes; and there shall be no more death, neither sorrow, nor crying, neither shall there be any more pain: for the former things are passed away" (Revelation 21:4, KJV).
The Bible's answer is partly a promise: the story is not finished.`,
    theological: `## Putting the answers together
1. **Free creatures and genuine love.** God created beings who can love freely, which requires the possibility of rejecting Him. Moral evil arises from the abuse of that freedom. Many Christian thinkers (Augustine, Aquinas, Lewis and Plantinga) have developed this "free will defence".
2. **Sin affects creation.** Natural evil belongs to a world under the curse (Romans 8:20–22), awaiting restoration. We should not claim to explain every particular disaster.
3. **God uses evil without endorsing it.** Joseph, the cross and the deliverance of Israel show that God can accomplish good through what humans and Satan meant for harm. The cross is the greatest evil ever committed and the greatest good ever accomplished: "Him, being delivered by the determinate counsel and foreknowledge of God, ye have taken, and by wicked hands have crucified and slain" (Acts 2:23, KJV).
4. **God shares our suffering.** The cross is God's answer in action. The Creator did not remain distant; the Son entered the world and bore its pain.
5. **Suffering can shape and purify** (Romans 5:3–5; Hebrews 12:10–11), without implying that every pain has a purpose we can name.
6. **The ultimate answer is the end of evil.** God will judge all injustice and remove death.
7. **Limits of our knowledge.** "The secret things belong unto the LORD our God: but those things which are revealed belong unto us" (Deuteronomy 29:29, KJV).

## What we should not say
- "Everything happens for a reason" as a glib comfort.
- "You must have done something wrong."
- "God needed another angel."
Instead, show compassion and honesty.`,
    views: `Christians differ about how God's control relates to human freedom:
- **Reformed** theologians emphasise God's meticulous sovereignty: nothing happens outside His plan, yet He is not the author of sin, and humans remain responsible.
- **Arminian and Wesleyan** theologians emphasise that God gives real freedom and that He permits genuine evil.
- **Open theists** hold that God does not exhaustively know the future; most evangelicals reject this.
- **Eastern Orthodox** theology emphasises the fall and healing of creation through the incarnation.
- **Greater-good** and **soul-making** approaches stress the role of suffering in forming character.
All traditions affirm that God is good, evil is real, the cross is God's answer and evil will end.`,
    pastoral: `When someone is hurting, do not begin with explanations. Sit with them. Read a psalm of lament (Psalm 13, 22, 73 or 88). Allow questions and anger. Say, "I don't know why, but I know God is near and that He cares." Share the story of Jesus weeping and the promise of the new creation. Offer practical help. For those who ask the question as a challenge, treat it as a serious intellectual and personal issue.` },

  { slug: "why-doesnt-god-just-kill-satan", category: "God, evil and judgement", featured: true,
    title: "Why does God not just kill Satan?",
    summary: "The Bible says God has already judged and defeated Satan at the cross and has fixed the time of his final destruction (Revelation 20:10). Until then God permits him a limited, supervised activity within His wider purposes: to complete the harvest of the gospel, to expose and judge evil fully and fairly, to show God's wisdom to the powers, and to give time for repentance. Satan is not God's equal and has no power that God does not allow.",
    scriptures: ["Genesis 3:15", "Job 1:6-12", "Job 2:1-6", "Matthew 8:28-29", "Matthew 13:24-30", "Luke 10:17-20", "John 12:31", "Colossians 2:13-15", "Hebrews 2:14-15", "1 John 3:8", "Revelation 12:7-12", "Revelation 20:1-10", "2 Peter 3:8-9"],
    readings: ["Michael Heiser, Unseen Realm (on the divine council; read critically)", "Clinton Arnold, Powers of Darkness", "Gregory Boyd, God at War (a different view; read critically)", "Augustine, City of God, Book 11"],
    exegetical: `## What the Bible says about Satan
**He is a created being.** All things in heaven and earth were created by and for Christ, "whether they be thrones, or dominions, or principalities, or powers" (Colossians 1:16, KJV). He is not a rival god or an eternal opposite of God. He is a creature who rebelled (John 8:44; 1 John 3:8; Jude 6; 2 Peter 2:4).

**He is accountable to God.** In Job 1:6–12 Satan appears among "the sons of God" before the LORD. God sets limits: "Behold, all that he hath is in thy power; only upon himself put not forth thine hand" (Job 1:12, KJV). In Job 2:6 God says, "Behold, he is in thine hand; but save his life." In Luke 22:31 Satan has "desired to have you, that he may sift you as wheat" and Jesus prays for Peter. In 1 Corinthians 10:13 God does not permit temptation beyond what we can bear. Satan has no power that God does not allow.

**He is already judged.** Jesus said, "Now is the judgment of this world: now shall the prince of this world be cast out" (John 12:31, KJV). "I beheld Satan as lightning fall from heaven" (Luke 10:18, KJV). On the cross Christ "spoiled principalities and powers, he made a shew of them openly, triumphing over them in it" (Colossians 2:15, KJV). The Son took flesh "that through death he might destroy him that had the power of death, that is, the devil" (Hebrews 2:14, KJV). "For this purpose the Son of God was manifested, that he might destroy the works of the devil" (1 John 3:8, KJV).

**His final end is certain.** "And the devil that deceived them was cast into the lake of fire and brimstone... and shall be tormented day and night for ever and ever" (Revelation 20:10, KJV). Revelation 12:11 says believers "overcame him by the blood of the Lamb, and by the word of their testimony".

## "Before the time"
When Jesus met two demon-possessed men, the demons cried out: "What have we to do with thee, Jesus, thou Son of God? art thou come hither to torment us before the time?" (Matthew 8:29, KJV). Even the demons know there is an appointed time of judgement. The question is therefore not whether God will deal with Satan, but when.

## Reasons the Bible gives for the delay
1. **The patience of God.** "The Lord is not slack concerning his promise, as some men count slackness; but is longsuffering to us-ward, not willing that any should perish, but that all should come to repentance" (2 Peter 3:9, KJV). The end of the age brings the end of the opportunity to repent.
2. **Wheat and weeds together.** In the parable of the tares, the master says, "Let both grow together until the harvest" (Matthew 13:30, KJV), lest the wheat be uprooted with the weeds. Judgement is delayed so that the harvest may be gathered.
3. **Evil must be fully exposed.** God does not act arbitrarily. The history of the world, the cross and the final judgement will demonstrate that God's judgements are just. "Great and marvellous are thy works, Lord God Almighty; just and true are thy ways" (Revelation 15:3, KJV).
4. **The wisdom of God shown to the powers.** Paul says that "unto the principalities and powers in heavenly places might be known by the church the manifold wisdom of God" (Ephesians 3:10, KJV).
5. **The cross as victory.** God chose to defeat the devil not by raw power but by the self-giving of the Son. "Having spoiled principalities and powers..." (Colossians 2:15).`,
    archaeological: `## The ancient world's view of cosmic evil
- **Canaanite and Mesopotamian myths** (from tablets at Ugarit, about 1400–1200 BC, and the Babylonian *Enuma Elish*) present rivalry between gods and chaos monsters (Baal against Yam; Marduk against Tiamat) in which the victory of the god is uncertain and must be won repeatedly. The Bible uses some of the same imagery (Leviathan, the sea, Rahab; Psalm 74:13–14; Isaiah 27:1) but with a radical difference: there is no rival to God; the sea monsters are His creatures and are under His control (Job 41; Psalm 104:26).
- **Persian dualism.** Zoroastrian thought, known from Persian-period sources, pictures two co-equal spirits in cosmic conflict. Israel's Scripture does not share this: God alone is Creator and sovereign (Isaiah 45:5–7).
- **Qumran.** The Dead Sea Scrolls (for example the *War Scroll*) speak of Belial as the leader of the forces of darkness, showing that many Jews around the time of Jesus expected a final victory of God over Belial. The New Testament presents that victory as already won in the cross and awaiting completion.
No excavation discusses Satan directly. What archaeology provides is the religious setting that shows how distinctive the Bible's teaching is.`,
    apostolic: `The apostles proclaimed a victorious Christ and warned believers to stand firm:
- **Paul:** "And the God of peace shall bruise Satan under your feet shortly" (Romans 16:20, KJV).
- **Paul on spiritual warfare:** "For we wrestle not against flesh and blood, but against principalities, against powers, against the rulers of the darkness of this world" (Ephesians 6:12, KJV). The armour of God (6:10–18).
- **Peter:** "Be sober, be vigilant; because your adversary the devil, as a roaring lion, walketh about, seeking whom he may devour: Whom resist stedfast in the faith" (1 Peter 5:8–9, KJV).
- **James:** "Resist the devil, and he will flee from you" (James 4:7, KJV).
- **John:** "greater is he that is in you, than he that is in the world" (1 John 4:4, KJV).`,
    prophetic: `The first prophecy is Genesis 3:15: the offspring of the woman will bruise the serpent's head, while the serpent bruises his heel. Later prophetic passages (Isaiah 27:1; Zechariah 3:1–2; Daniel 7; Joel 3) anticipate the end of evil powers. Revelation shows the stages: Satan cast down from heaven (12:7–12), bound for a thousand years (20:1–3), released briefly (20:7–9), and finally cast into the lake of fire (20:10). Christians differ about whether the thousand years is literal and future or symbolic and present (see the question on the millennium).`,
    theological: `## Putting it together
1. **God could destroy Satan at any moment; His delay is purposeful, not weakness.** The question assumes that "killing" would solve the problem. Evil is not only an external force but a rebellion in which humans also share (James 1:14). If God destroyed every evildoer immediately, none of us would remain.
2. **Satan is defeated in principle.** The decisive battle was fought at Calvary and the resurrection. The remaining period is like the time between D-Day and VE-Day: the outcome is settled, but fighting continues.
3. **God brings good out of Satan's schemes.** The betrayal and crucifixion of Jesus were Satan's work (John 13:2, 27; 1 Corinthians 2:8), yet they accomplished salvation.
4. **God wants a world in which His justice is seen to be perfect.** A premature, unexplained destruction would leave questions; the final judgement will leave none.
5. **Believers share in the victory.** We are not victims of a cosmic power struggle but participants in Christ's triumph (Romans 8:37).

## An important caution
Do not blame every problem on Satan; the Bible also speaks of our own desires and the world's ways (James 1:14; 1 John 2:16). Equally, do not ignore the spiritual dimension of life. The balanced position: take the enemy seriously, and take Christ more seriously.`,
    views: `- **God permits Satan for the sake of a greater good** (the classical view): to allow freedom, to complete the harvest and to display His glory.
- **A warfare model** (for example Gregory Boyd) sees creation as a place of real conflict in which God limits Himself to work through persuasion and the cross. Most evangelicals think it underplays God's sovereignty.
- **Satan's identity and fall.** Many identify Isaiah 14 and Ezekiel 28 with Satan's fall, others see them as describing earthly kings (see the question on Lucifer).
- **Annihilation versus eternal torment** of Satan and demons: Revelation 20:10 is read by most as eternal punishment.`,
    pastoral: `For a person afraid of Satan: remind them that Christ has already won, that the devil can only do what God permits, and that believers are sealed by the Spirit. Encourage prayer, Scripture, community and repentance. For a person who asks, "Why doesn't God just stop him?", acknowledge the depth of the question, point to the cross and speak of the day when "the God of peace shall bruise Satan under your feet".` },

  { slug: "who-is-lucifer-isaiah-14-ezekiel-28", category: "God, evil and judgement",
    title: "Who is Lucifer? Do Isaiah 14 and Ezekiel 28 describe Satan's fall?",
    summary: "The name Lucifer comes from the Latin Vulgate's rendering of a Hebrew word in Isaiah 14:12 meaning 'shining one'. In context Isaiah is mocking the king of Babylon, and Ezekiel is lamenting the king of Tyre. Many Christians through history have also seen echoes of the fall of the great adversary behind these kings; others do not. The Bible's clear teaching on Satan's origin and fall does not depend on these two chapters.",
    scriptures: ["Isaiah 14:3-21", "Ezekiel 28:1-19", "Luke 10:18", "John 8:44", "1 Timothy 3:6", "2 Peter 2:4", "Jude 1:6", "Revelation 12:7-9"],
    readings: ["John Oswalt, The Book of Isaiah, Chapters 1–39 (NICOT)", "Daniel Block, The Book of Ezekiel, Chapters 25–48 (NICOT)", "Michael Heiser, Unseen Realm", "Tertullian and Origen on Isaiah 14 (early interpretations)"],
    exegetical: `## Isaiah 14: the king of Babylon
The chapter opens with a "proverb" (a taunt) against "the king of Babylon" (Isaiah 14:4). The picture is of a tyrant brought down to Sheol, where the dead mock him: "Art thou also become weak as we? art thou become like unto us?" (14:10, KJV). Then: "How art thou fallen from heaven, O Lucifer, son of the morning! how art thou cut down to the ground, which didst weaken the nations!" (14:12, KJV). "For thou hast said in thine heart, I will ascend into heaven, I will exalt my throne above the stars of God... I will be like the most High" (14:13–14, KJV). The chapter ends with the king unburied and his descendants cut off (14:19–21).

## The word "Lucifer"
The Hebrew is *helel ben shachar* (H1966): "shining one, son of the dawn", a name for the morning star (Venus). The Greek Septuagint translated it *heōsphoros* ("dawn-bearer") and Jerome's Latin Vulgate used *lucifer* ("light-bearer"), which became a name in English translations, including the KJV. It is a poetic title, not a personal name for the devil in Scripture.

## Ezekiel 28: the king of Tyre
Ezekiel's lament is also addressed to a human ruler: "Son of man, say unto the prince of Tyrus" (Ezekiel 28:2, KJV) who said, "I am a God, I sit in the seat of God" (28:2). In 28:11–19 the lament uses Eden imagery: "Thou hast been in Eden the garden of God; every precious stone was thy covering... Thou art the anointed cherub that covereth; and I have set thee so: thou wast upon the holy mountain of God" (Ezekiel 28:13–14, KJV). "Thou wast perfect in thy ways from the day that thou wast created, till iniquity was found in thee" (Ezekiel 28:15, KJV). The being is cast out from "the mountain of God" (28:16).

## Two ways of reading
1. **Primarily the human kings, using mythic and cosmic imagery.** Both passages are introduced as taunts against kings. The imagery (ascending to heaven, being thrown down; Eden and the cherub) is the language of pride and judgement. Isaiah 14 echoes ancient myths of a star god who tries to rise above the gods. The prophets use the language to say: your pride is the same pride as the primeval rebel's, and you will fall.
2. **Double reference: the human king and, behind him, the satanic power.** Early Christian writers (Origen, Tertullian, later Augustine) saw Satan's fall in these texts. Their reasoning: some details fit no human king (a "cherub", being in Eden, "perfect from the day thou wast created"); Satan is "the prince of this world", who stands behind earthly tyrants (Daniel 10:13, 20 speaks of the "prince of Persia").

## What the New Testament says directly
- "And he said unto them, I beheld Satan as lightning fall from heaven" (Luke 10:18, KJV).
- Satan "was a murderer from the beginning, and abode not in the truth" (John 8:44).
- A novice must not be lifted up with pride "lest being lifted up with pride he fall into the condemnation of the devil" (1 Timothy 3:6, KJV).
- God "spared not the angels that sinned, but cast them down to hell" (2 Peter 2:4, KJV); "the angels which kept not their first estate" (Jude 6, KJV).
- Revelation 12:7–9 describes war in heaven and the dragon thrown down.
So the doctrine of the fall of the devil rests on several texts; Isaiah 14 and Ezekiel 28 may illustrate it without being its only basis.`,
    genealogical: `Babylon and Tyre were real kingdoms, and their kings are historical figures. Isaiah is probably speaking of Assyrian or Babylonian rulers (Sargon II, Sennacherib or Nebuchadnezzar). Ezekiel addresses Ithobaal II of Tyre (about 590–573 BC), who is named in Babylonian records. The "cherub" language and Eden imagery are not genealogical statements about them.`,
    archaeological: `- **Babylonian and Assyrian royal boasting.** Royal inscriptions regularly claim divine status or divine favour, such as Sennacherib's claim to have "scaled the heights of Lebanon". Nebuchadnezzar's inscriptions glorify his building. This explains the proud speech of Isaiah 14:13–14.
- **Ugaritic myth.** A text from Ras Shamra tells of Athtar, a minor god, who tries to occupy Baal's throne on Mount Zaphon and is found too small for it. Some think that Isaiah echoes a similar tradition about the morning star; this is debated.
- **Tyre.** Excavations at Tyre and Phoenician inscriptions show the wealth and trade of the island city, matching Ezekiel's description in chapters 27–28. The Eden and jewelled "garden" imagery has parallels with ancient Near Eastern temple-garden motifs.
- **Babylon.** Excavations of the city of Babylon (the Ishtar Gate, processional way, palace) show the splendour that Isaiah says will fall. Babylon was captured by Cyrus in 539 BC (the Nabonidus Chronicle; Cyrus Cylinder).`,
    apostolic: `The apostles speak of pride as the devil's sin (1 Timothy 3:6) and of Satan masquerading: "for Satan himself is transformed into an angel of light" (2 Corinthians 11:14, KJV). They teach believers to be humble (James 4:6–7; 1 Peter 5:5–8), because pride led to his fall.`,
    prophetic: `The pattern that "everyone who exalts himself shall be abased" (Luke 14:11) and that proud empires fall runs through prophecy. Babylon in Revelation 17–18 is the symbol of proud world power, judged in the end. The "king of Babylon" and "king of Tyre" texts remind us that God judges rulers who claim divinity.`,
    theological: `## Taking the texts seriously without overreaching
- **Respect the context.** These are taunts and laments against real kings. We should not build a full biography of Satan from them.
- **Allow depth of meaning.** The prophets sometimes speak of historical figures in language that points beyond them. Christian tradition has found it fitting to hear Satan's pride in these taunts.
- **Hold fast to what is certain:** God created all things good; a creature rebelled; pride is the root of the fall; the devil is judged and will be destroyed.
- **Warn against extra-biblical stories.** Many books and songs go beyond Scripture with names, ranks and details of angelic history. Test them.`,
    views: `- **Satan's fall in view (traditional view):** held by Origen, Tertullian, Jerome and many Protestants including Calvin's and Luther's circles in part.
- **Only earthly kings (many modern commentators):** the texts do not refer to Satan, though the New Testament and other passages teach his fall.
- **Mediating view:** the passages primarily denounce human kings, with a sense of typology linked to the primeval pride of the adversary.
Charitable disagreement is appropriate.`,
    pastoral: `Avoid building fear or fascination around angelic hierarchies. Use this passage to warn about pride and to point to Christ, who "humbled himself" and was exalted (Philippians 2:5–11).` },

  { slug: "sons-of-god-and-the-nephilim-genesis-6", category: "God, evil and judgement",
    title: "Who were the 'sons of God' and the Nephilim in Genesis 6?",
    summary: "Three main interpretations exist: (1) fallen angels who married human women, (2) the godly line of Seth who intermarried with the line of Cain, and (3) ancient tyrant kings. Each has strong supporters. The text's moral point is clear: humanity's corruption grew to the point of judgement by the flood.",
    scriptures: ["Genesis 6:1-8", "Genesis 4:16-26", "Job 1:6", "Job 38:7", "Psalms 82:1-8", "Numbers 13:33", "Jude 1:6-7", "2 Peter 2:4-5", "Matthew 22:30"],
    readings: ["Gordon Wenham, Genesis 1–15 (Word Biblical Commentary)", "Kenneth Mathews, Genesis 1–11:26 (NAC)", "Michael Heiser, The Unseen Realm", "Derek Kidner, Genesis (Tyndale)"],
    exegetical: `## The text
"And it came to pass, when men began to multiply on the face of the earth, and daughters were born unto them, That the sons of God saw the daughters of men that they were fair; and they took them wives of all which they chose" (Genesis 6:1–2, KJV). "There were giants in the earth in those days; and also after that, when the sons of God came in unto the daughters of men, and they bare children to them, the same became mighty men which were of old, men of renown" (Genesis 6:4, KJV). The Hebrew word for "giants" is *nephilim* (H5303), probably from a verb meaning to fall.

## Three interpretations
**1. The sons of God are fallen angels.** "Sons of God" (*bene ha-elohim*) refers to angels in Job 1:6, 2:1 and 38:7 (compare Psalm 89:6; Daniel 3:25). The Jewish book of 1 Enoch (a non-biblical work from the third to first century BC) and early Jewish interpreters understood Genesis 6 in this way, and the New Testament letters of Jude 6–7 and 2 Peter 2:4–5 are often read as alluding to this tradition: "the angels which kept not their first estate, but left their own habitation, he hath reserved in everlasting chains under darkness" (Jude 6, KJV). Objection: Jesus says angels "neither marry, nor are given in marriage" (Matthew 22:30), though that speaks of angels in heaven, not necessarily fallen ones on earth.

**2. The sons of God are the godly line of Seth.** Genesis 4–5 traces Cain's line (4:17–24) and Seth's line, with whom people began "to call upon the name of the LORD" (4:26). Many Christians, including Augustine, Calvin and many Protestants, hold that the "sons of God" are believers who intermarried with unbelievers, with disastrous results. Objection: nothing in the text calls the Sethites "sons of God", and giants born of such unions seem odd. Hosea 1:10 calls Israelites "sons of the living God".

**3. The sons of God are human rulers.** In the ancient Near East kings were called sons of gods, and the passage may refer to powerful rulers who took many wives by force (a view held by some Jewish rabbis). The mighty men "of renown" would then be tyrants.

## The Nephilim
The text says the Nephilim were "in the earth in those days; and also after that". They are not said to be the children of the union (Genesis 6:4 can be read as saying that they were on earth then, and that the offspring of the unions were the "mighty men"). In Numbers 13:33 the scouts claim that the Anakites were descendants of the Nephilim, though the scouts' report was a faithless exaggeration.

## The moral emphasis
Whichever interpretation one holds, the passage's point is stated in verse 5: "And God saw that the wickedness of man was great in the earth, and that every imagination of the thoughts of his heart was only evil continually" (Genesis 6:5, KJV). Humanity was filled with violence (6:11) and the result was the flood.`,
    genealogical: `Genesis 5 gives the line from Adam to Noah through Seth: Adam, Seth, Enosh, Kenan, Mahalalel, Jared, Enoch, Methuselah, Lamech, Noah. Genesis 4:17–24 gives Cain's line: Enoch, Irad, Mehujael, Methushael, Lamech with his sons Jabal, Jubal and Tubal-cain. The similarities of names show that Genesis interwove the two lines. The "Sethite" interpretation views Genesis 6 as the point where the lines merged.`,
    archaeological: `- **Ancient Near Eastern ideas of divine-human offspring.** Mesopotamian and Canaanite texts speak of semi-divine heroes (Gilgamesh was two-thirds divine) and kings claiming descent from gods. The *Sumerian King List* credits early kings with reigns of tens of thousands of years, an echo of primeval tradition.
- **1 Enoch.** Fragments of the Book of Enoch in Aramaic were found among the Dead Sea Scrolls (Cave 4), showing that the angelic interpretation was common before the time of Jesus. This does not make it correct; 1 Enoch is not Scripture. Jude 14–15 quotes it once, as the New Testament authors sometimes quote non-biblical works, without endorsing everything in it.
- **Giants.** Large-bodied peoples such as the Anakim and Rephaim are recorded in Canaan (Deuteronomy 2:10–11; 3:11). Archaeology records that the Philistines included tall people; the skeleton of an unusually tall man from Gath (not conclusive) has been noted. No excavation confirms "giants" in the sense of the Nephilim.`,
    apostolic: `Peter and Jude treat the angelic rebellion and the flood as warnings: "For if God spared not the angels that sinned, but cast them down to hell, and delivered them into chains of darkness, to be reserved unto judgment; And spared not the old world, but saved Noah the eighth person" (2 Peter 2:4–5, KJV). Their emphasis is on judgement and on God's care for the righteous. They do not go into detail about marriages.`,
    prophetic: `Jesus said, "as the days of Noe were, so shall also the coming of the Son of man be" (Matthew 24:37, KJV). The days of Noah were days of violence, corruption and indifference to God's warning. The passage serves as a warning of final judgement and a call to faithfulness. The "giants" motif (Goliath and the Philistine champions, 1 Samuel 17) is a reminder that the people of God confront powers that seem too large.`,
    theological: `## Responsible conclusions
- **Be modest.** Faithful scholars differ, and the text is brief. We should not build doctrine on speculation.
- **Heed the main message.** God judges widespread wickedness but preserves a righteous remnant (Noah found grace, 6:8).
- **Avoid sensationalism.** Many modern books use Genesis 6 for unfounded claims about aliens or genetic engineering. They are not based on the text.
- **Affirm the biblical teaching on angels:** they are created spirits who serve God, and some rebelled.`,
    views: `The three views are all held by sincere Christians. The angelic view has the strongest linguistic and Jewish background support; the Sethite view has long-standing Christian tradition behind it; the royal view is held by some Jewish and Christian scholars. Whichever view is taken, the flood narrative stands as a record of divine judgement.`,
    pastoral: `Do not allow a debate on this passage to distract from the gospel. If a friend asks about aliens or fallen angels in popular media, explain that Genesis 6 is brief and open to several readings, and that the Bible's focus is on the fall of humanity and God's salvation.` },

  { slug: "canaanite-conquest-was-god-unjust", category: "God, evil and judgement",
    title: "Was God unjust to command the conquest of Canaan?",
    summary: "The Bible presents the conquest as a unique, time-limited act of divine judgement on a long-standing, deeply corrupt society, with warnings and delay, and not as a model for later violence. The language of total destruction is largely conventional ancient war rhetoric, many Canaanites remained, and some were spared. Christians do not use it to justify violence; Jesus ends the use of the sword for the kingdom.",
    scriptures: ["Genesis 15:13-16", "Leviticus 18:24-30", "Deuteronomy 7:1-6", "Deuteronomy 9:1-6", "Deuteronomy 20:10-18", "Joshua 2:1-14", "Joshua 6:22-25", "Judges 1:1-36", "Matthew 26:51-52", "John 18:36"],
    readings: ["Paul Copan and Matthew Flannagan, Did God Really Command Genocide?", "Eugene Merrill, Deuteronomy (NAC)", "Christopher J. H. Wright, The God I Don't Understand (chapter on the conquest)", "Peter Enns and Kenton Sparks on different approaches (read critically)"],
    exegetical: `## What the Bible says
God told Abraham that his descendants would possess the land "in the fourth generation... for the iniquity of the Amorites is not yet full" (Genesis 15:16, KJV). God's judgement was delayed for centuries. Before the conquest Moses declared: "Speak not thou in thine heart... saying, For my righteousness the LORD hath brought me in to possess this land: but for the wickedness of these nations the LORD doth drive them out from before thee... Not for thy righteousness... But for the wickedness of these nations" (Deuteronomy 9:4–5, KJV). The land "vomiteth out her inhabitants" for abominations including child sacrifice (Leviticus 18:24–30; Deuteronomy 12:31; 18:9–12).

## The language of "destroy all"
Texts such as Joshua 10:40 and 11:20 speak of destroying everything that breathed, yet the same book shows that Canaanites remained (Joshua 15:63; 16:10; 17:12) and the book of Judges describes continuing Canaanite presence (Judges 1). The language belongs to a conventional style of ancient warfare reports in which "utterly destroyed" and "all" are used as hyperbole (Egyptian, Hittite and Moabite inscriptions use the same style, for example the claim of the Moabite Stone that "Israel has perished for ever", though Israel continued to exist). Joshua 10:40 uses the language and then in 15:13–19 shows Judah living among them.

## The *herem* (devotion to destruction)
The term *herem* (H2764, "the ban") means setting something apart for God, often by destruction. It was applied to a specific group of cities for a limited purpose: to prevent the corruption of Israel (Deuteronomy 7:4; 20:18). Many of the cities in Joshua were military centres or fortified towns rather than settlements of civilians (Jericho, Ai, Hazor). The text mostly speaks of "kings" and "cities".

## Mercy within judgement
- **Rahab** and her family were spared because she feared the LORD (Joshua 2; 6:25).
- **The Gibeonites** were allowed to live under treaty (Joshua 9).
- **The offer of peace.** Deuteronomy 20:10 requires an offer of peace before attack.
- Israel's own sin brought judgement on Israel as well: Achan's sin (Joshua 7), and the exile of Israel and Judah, "even as the nations" (2 Kings 17:7–18).

## Not a model for others
The command was unique to Israel's entry into the land under Moses and Joshua. The New Testament never uses it as a precedent. Jesus told Peter, "Put up again thy sword into his place: for all they that take the sword shall perish with the sword" (Matthew 26:52, KJV), and told Pilate, "My kingdom is not of this world: if my kingdom were of this world, then would my servants fight" (John 18:36, KJV).`,
    genealogical: `Genesis 10 records Canaan as a son of Ham (10:6, 15–19), with the peoples that were later in the land: the Jebusites, Amorites, Girgashites, Hivites, Arkites, Sinites, Arvadites, Zemarites and Hamathites. Genesis 15:16 says that the Amorites' wickedness would be complete after four generations (or four hundred years, 15:13). The Anakim and Rephaim were large, warlike peoples in the land.`,
    archaeological: `## What excavations show
- **Canaanite religion.** Texts from Ugarit (about 1400–1200 BC) describe the cults of Baal, Anat, Asherah and El. Excavations at Canaanite sites such as Gezer, Megiddo and Hazor have uncovered high places and cultic objects. Evidence of child sacrifice (burials of infants in urns) is clear at the later Phoenician and Carthaginian *tophets*, and the practice of child sacrifice in Canaan is discussed in Deuteronomy 12:31; scholars debate how widespread it was in the late bronze age.
- **Destruction layers.** Archaeologists have found burnt destruction levels at Hazor (late thirteenth century BC), and at other sites, but they disagree about the dates and about who caused them. Egyptian campaigns, Sea Peoples, local conflict and earthquakes are all possible causes.
- **Jericho and Ai.** Garstang (1930s) dated a collapsed wall at Jericho to about 1400 BC; Kenyon (1950s) dated the destruction much earlier (about 2300 BC) and found little occupation in the late bronze period; Bryant Wood has argued that Garstang's date should be favoured; the debate continues. For Ai, the traditional site (et-Tell) was uninhabited in the late bronze age, so some propose other sites (Khirbet el-Maqatir, Bethel). We should state honestly that archaeology has not resolved these questions.
- **Gradual change.** Many scholars see archaeological evidence for a gradual emergence of Israel in the central hills (new villages from about 1200 BC), which is consistent with the book of Judges' picture of infiltration, conflict and coexistence rather than a quick total conquest.
- **Merneptah Stele.** The Egyptian stele of about 1208 BC lists "Israel" among peoples in Canaan, evidence that Israel was a people in the land by the late thirteenth century.`,
    apostolic: `The apostles read the conquest as part of salvation history: Hebrews 11:30–31 mentions the fall of Jericho and Rahab's faith. Stephen's sermon recalls God's driving out of nations before Israel (Acts 7:45). The apostles insist that Christ's followers fight "not against flesh and blood" (Ephesians 6:12), and they endure persecution without taking up arms (Romans 12:17–21).`,
    prophetic: `The conquest points to the final judgement and deliverance, when God will remove evil and give His people rest. The prophets warn Israel that if it behaves like the Canaanites it will suffer the same fate (Jeremiah 7; Amos 2). Rahab, a Canaanite, appears in the line of Jesus (Matthew 1:5), a sign that grace reaches even the condemned.`,
    theological: `## Responding to the objection
1. **God is the righteous Judge.** He has the right to give and take life; to judge nations for persistent evil; and to do so in a way He appoints. We cannot assume that the judgement was arbitrary.
2. **The delay shows patience.** God waited for 400 years (Genesis 15:16).
3. **The execution was limited and uniquely commissioned.** It does not authorise anyone today to wage religious war. The New Testament marks a change: the church advances by witness, not the sword.
4. **We must read texts honestly.** The Bible does record violence. Some passages are hard to understand. We should take them seriously, avoid trivialising them and avoid using them to justify violence.
5. **God's character is displayed at the cross.** In Jesus, God bears the cost of judgement Himself.

## What we should not say
- That God's commands in Joshua are a model for the church.
- That the issue is easy to resolve.
- That anyone who is troubled lacks faith. Honest wrestling is part of Scripture itself (Habakkuk, Job, Psalms).`,
    views: `Christians differ in how they handle this issue:
- **The traditional view:** these were commanded acts of judgement on wicked nations, limited in time, and hyperbolic in the report.
- **The hyperbole and limited-scope view:** the language is ancient rhetoric; the main targets were military and political centres.
- **The accommodation or "cross-shaped" reading:** some theologians suggest that Israel's writers interpreted events through the conventions of their time, and that the full revelation of God's character is in Christ. This view is controversial and not held by those who affirm strict inerrancy.
All agree that the conquest provides no warrant for violence today.`,
    pastoral: `When someone is troubled, admit the difficulty, share what the text says and does not say, and invite them to look at Jesus. Do not offer slogans. Remember that many have suffered in the name of religion; we must acknowledge this and speak of God's justice and mercy with humility.` },

  { slug: "predestination-and-free-will", category: "God, evil and judgement",
    title: "Predestination or free will? Does God choose who is saved?",
    summary: "The Bible teaches both God's sovereign choice and human responsibility to repent and believe, and it never presents them as opposed. Christians have explained the relationship in different ways (Calvinist, Arminian and others). All agree that salvation is by grace alone, that God desires all to be saved, and that no one who comes to Christ is turned away.",
    scriptures: ["Romans 8:28-30", "Romans 9:1-33", "Ephesians 1:3-14", "John 6:35-44", "John 3:16-18", "1 Timothy 2:1-6", "2 Peter 3:9", "Acts 13:48", "Acts 17:30-31", "Philippians 2:12-13"],
    readings: ["J. I. Packer, Evangelism and the Sovereignty of God", "D. A. Carson, Divine Sovereignty and Human Responsibility", "Roger Olson, Arminian Theology: Myths and Realities", "Thomas Schreiner and Bruce Ware (eds.), The Grace of God, the Bondage of the Will"],
    exegetical: `## Texts that stress God's initiative
- "Blessed be the God and Father of our Lord Jesus Christ... According as he hath chosen us in him before the foundation of the world, that we should be holy and without blame before him in love: Having predestinated us unto the adoption of children by Jesus Christ to himself" (Ephesians 1:3–5, KJV).
- "For whom he did foreknow, he also did predestinate to be conformed to the image of his Son... Moreover whom he did predestinate, them he also called: and whom he called, them he also justified: and whom he justified, them he also glorified" (Romans 8:29–30, KJV).
- "No man can come to me, except the Father which hath sent me draw him" (John 6:44, KJV).
- "And as many as were ordained to eternal life believed" (Acts 13:48, KJV).
- Romans 9 speaks of Jacob and Esau, the potter and the clay and God's mercy "on whom he will have mercy".

## Texts that stress human responsibility and God's desire to save all
- "For God so loved the world, that he gave his only begotten Son, that whosoever believeth in him should not perish" (John 3:16, KJV).
- God "will have all men to be saved, and to come unto the knowledge of the truth" (1 Timothy 2:4, KJV).
- "The Lord is... not willing that any should perish, but that all should come to repentance" (2 Peter 3:9, KJV).
- "And the times of this ignorance God winked at; but now commandeth all men every where to repent" (Acts 17:30, KJV).
- "him that cometh to me I will in no wise cast out" (John 6:37, KJV).
- "Work out your own salvation with fear and trembling. For it is God which worketh in you both to will and to do of his good pleasure" (Philippians 2:12–13, KJV).

## Romans 9–11 in context
Paul wrestles with why many in Israel have not believed. He affirms God's freedom (9:15–18) and human responsibility (10:9–13, 21). His conclusion is worship: "O the depth of the riches both of the wisdom and knowledge of God! how unsearchable are his judgments, and his ways past finding out!" (Romans 11:33, KJV). In Romans 11:32 he says "God hath concluded them all in unbelief, that he might have mercy upon all".

## What "foreknow" means
Romans 8:29 says "foreknow". Calvinists understand it as God's knowing in love and choosing beforehand (compare "known" in Amos 3:2; Jeremiah 1:5). Arminians understand it as God's foreseeing who would believe. Both hold that salvation begins with grace.`,
    archaeological: `The question is theological, but the historical background helps:
- **Jewish teaching about election.** The Dead Sea Scrolls (the *Community Rule*) speak of the "sons of light" and "sons of darkness" and of God's predestined plan, showing that the question was debated in Judaism at the time of Jesus. Josephus says that the Pharisees held both fate (God's providence) and human freedom, while the Sadducees denied fate, and the Essenes held that everything is determined by God (*Antiquities* 13.171–173).
- **Greek and Roman ideas of fate.** Stoic fate (*heimarmene*) contrasts with the biblical teaching of a personal God whose purposes are good and whose will can be sought in prayer.`,
    apostolic: `Paul teaches election as a ground for confidence and worship, not for speculation: "What shall we then say to these things? If God be for us, who can be against us?" (Romans 8:31, KJV). He also pleads with sinners: "we pray you in Christ's stead, be ye reconciled to God" (2 Corinthians 5:20, KJV). Peter writes to those "elect according to the foreknowledge of God the Father" (1 Peter 1:2) and calls them to holiness. The apostles preached to all and trusted God to bring in His chosen.`,
    prophetic: `The prophets also speak of God's sovereign choice (Isaiah 46:9–11: "My counsel shall stand, and I will do all my pleasure") and of the invitation to all (Isaiah 55:1, "Ho, every one that thirsteth, come ye to the waters"). Ezekiel 18:23 asks: "Have I any pleasure at all that the wicked should die?... and not that he should return from his ways, and live?" (KJV).`,
    theological: `## Two main traditions
**Calvinist (Reformed):** God, before creation, chose some to be saved by grace alone (unconditional election); Christ died to secure their salvation; the Spirit effectively calls them; they will persevere. Human responsibility remains real. The motive is to give all glory to God.

**Arminian (Wesleyan):** God gives prevenient grace to all, enabling them to respond; election is based on God's foreknowledge of faith; Christ died for all; believers can resist grace and may fall away. The motive is to defend God's love for all and the reality of human response.

**Others** (Lutheran, Catholic, Orthodox, Molinist) offer variations.

## Common ground
1. Salvation is entirely by grace through Christ.
2. God desires the salvation of people and commands everyone to repent.
3. The gospel should be preached to all.
4. No one who truly seeks Christ is rejected.
5. Believers are secure in Christ's love and are called to persevere.
6. God is not the author of sin and the damned are responsible for their rejection.

## Tension and mystery
The Bible holds divine sovereignty and human responsibility together without fully explaining how. Spurgeon is reported to have said that he never tried to reconcile friends. Both emphases comfort and motivate: sovereignty gives hope that God will save; responsibility gives urgency to preach and repent.`,
    views: `The debate is old (Augustine against Pelagius, Calvin and Arminius, Whitefield and Wesley). Faithful Christians in both camps have been mighty in evangelism and holiness. Avoid caricature: Calvinists are not fatalists and Arminians do not deny grace. Do not break fellowship over this issue.`,
    pastoral: `For an anxious person who fears they are not chosen: if you desire Christ and trust Him, that desire is itself evidence of God's work. Invite them to look to Christ, not to the decree. For a proud person: remember that salvation is a gift. For the evangelist: preach to all, pray for the Spirit's work, and rest in God's sovereignty.` },

  { slug: "hell-eternal-punishment-and-other-views", category: "God, evil and judgement",
    title: "What does the Bible teach about hell? Is it eternal?",
    summary: "The Bible teaches real, serious, final judgement and that Jesus spoke about it more than anyone. Most Christians through history have understood it as everlasting conscious punishment. A minority (conditionalists) hold that the unsaved are finally destroyed, and some argue for eventual universal restoration. Each view appeals to Scripture; the biblical call is to take judgement seriously, trust God's justice and mercy, and proclaim the gospel.",
    scriptures: ["Matthew 25:31-46", "Mark 9:42-48", "Luke 16:19-31", "2 Thessalonians 1:5-10", "Revelation 20:11-15", "Daniel 12:1-3", "Matthew 10:28", "Romans 6:23", "John 3:16-18", "2 Peter 3:9", "1 Timothy 2:4"],
    readings: ["Robert Peterson, Hell on Trial", "Edward Fudge, The Fire That Consumes", "Christopher Morgan and Robert Peterson (eds.), Hell Under Fire", "Four Views on Hell (Zondervan, William Crockett ed.)"],
    exegetical: `## Terms used
- **Sheol** (Hebrew) and **Hades** (Greek) usually refer to the realm of the dead (Psalm 16:10; Acts 2:27).
- **Gehenna** (Greek *geenna*, from the Valley of Hinnom outside Jerusalem, associated with child sacrifice in Jeremiah 7:31; 19:5–6) is used by Jesus for the place of final judgement (Matthew 5:22, 29; 10:28; 23:33; Mark 9:43–48).
- **Tartarus** appears once of the angels (2 Peter 2:4).
- **The lake of fire** (Revelation 19:20; 20:10, 14–15).

## What Jesus said
- "And fear not them which kill the body, but are not able to kill the soul: but rather fear him which is able to destroy both soul and body in hell" (Matthew 10:28, KJV).
- "And these shall go away into everlasting punishment: but the righteous into life eternal" (Matthew 25:46, KJV). The same Greek word *aiōnios* qualifies both punishment and life.
- Mark 9:48 describes "where their worm dieth not, and the fire is not quenched" (KJV), quoting Isaiah 66:24.
- The account of the rich man and Lazarus (Luke 16:19–31) describes conscious torment and a fixed gulf, though it is a parable and set before the resurrection.

## Apostolic texts
- "The wages of sin is death; but the gift of God is eternal life through Jesus Christ our Lord" (Romans 6:23, KJV).
- 2 Thessalonians 1:9: "Who shall be punished with everlasting destruction from the presence of the Lord, and from the glory of his power" (KJV).
- Revelation 14:10–11; 20:10, 15 describe torment and the lake of fire.
- Jude 7 compares Sodom and Gomorrah: "suffering the vengeance of eternal fire".

## Three understandings
1. **Everlasting conscious punishment (the traditional view).** "Everlasting punishment" and "torment... for ever and ever" mean what they say. The punishment is just because sin against an infinite God is infinitely serious (Anselm) and because the damned continue in rebellion.
2. **Conditional immortality or annihilationism.** Immortality is given only in Christ (1 Timothy 6:16; 2 Timothy 1:10); the wicked will be finally destroyed (Matthew 10:28; Psalm 37:20; Malachi 4:1–3; Philippians 3:19). The language of "destruction", "perish" and "death" is taken literally; "everlasting" refers to the result (an irreversible punishment). John Stott and Edward Fudge are among evangelicals who held this view tentatively.
3. **Universal restoration (universalism).** Texts such as 1 Timothy 2:4, Romans 5:18 and 1 Corinthians 15:22–28 are read as pointing to a final salvation of all (Origen; Gregory of Nyssa). The church condemned the doctrine in the sixth century and most Christians reject it because of Jesus' warnings and the finality of Revelation 20–22.`,
    archaeological: `- **Gehenna** was a real valley south of Jerusalem (the Hinnom Valley). Excavations in the area (for example the burial caves of Ketef Hinnom, with the silver scrolls of the Aaronic blessing, about 600 BC) show its use as a burial area; the Bible associates it with child sacrifice to Molech (2 Kings 23:10).
- **Jewish views of the afterlife.** Sources from the second temple period (1 Enoch, the Dead Sea Scrolls, Josephus, the Wisdom of Solomon) show a range of views: some expected punishment of the wicked after death in fire, others annihilation. Jesus' teaching draws on and goes beyond them.
- **Early Christian art and epitaphs** in the catacombs of Rome show confidence in resurrection and judgement, with images of the Good Shepherd and the Last Judgement later.`,
    apostolic: `The apostles preach the coming judgement as part of the gospel: "Because he hath appointed a day, in the which he will judge the world in righteousness by that man whom he hath ordained; whereof he hath given assurance unto all men, in that he hath raised him from the dead" (Acts 17:31, KJV). Paul reasons of "righteousness, temperance, and judgment to come" before Felix (Acts 24:25). Hebrews 9:27: "it is appointed unto men once to die, but after this the judgment." They urge believers to live holy lives and to "persuade men" (2 Corinthians 5:11).`,
    prophetic: `The prophets spoke of judgement: Isaiah 66:24 ("their worm shall not die, neither shall their fire be quenched"), Daniel 12:2 ("some to everlasting life, and some to shame and everlasting contempt"). The Lord's Day will be one of both salvation and judgement (Joel 2:31–32; Malachi 4:1–2). Revelation 20:11–15 shows the great white throne.`,
    theological: `## Key convictions
1. **God is perfectly just and perfectly merciful.** Hell is not what He desires: "Have I any pleasure at all that the wicked should die?" (Ezekiel 18:23). Yet He will not ignore evil.
2. **Hell is the consequence of rejecting God.** C. S. Lewis called it a locked door from the inside: those who refuse God choose their own end.
3. **Judgement is real and serious.** Jesus' warnings are meant to be heeded.
4. **Salvation is through Christ alone** (John 14:6; Acts 4:12), by grace through faith.
5. **We must be humble.** We do not know everything about the fate of the dead, and God's justice will be vindicated.

## Why this matters for ministry
- **Urgency:** the gospel is urgent.
- **Compassion:** do not speak of hell with delight.
- **Accuracy:** do not add details Scripture does not give (such as medieval imagery).
- **Hope:** Christ has borne the judgement for all who believe.`,
    views: `Evangelicals differ: most hold eternal conscious punishment; some, including John Stott, held conditional immortality as a possibility; a few hold universalism. The Catholic Church teaches the reality of hell and the possibility of purgatory for believers; Orthodox Christians speak of the fire of God's presence experienced as love or torment. The early church affirmed hell, with some universalist voices.`,
    pastoral: `Speak of hell seriously and gently. For someone grieving a loved one who may not have believed, avoid promising what Scripture does not promise; entrust the person to the Judge of all the earth who will do right (Genesis 18:25), and point to His mercy. For unbelievers, move to Jesus: He bore the cost.` },

  { slug: "what-about-those-who-never-heard", category: "God, evil and judgement",
    title: "What about people who have never heard the gospel?",
    summary: "The Bible teaches that salvation is through Christ alone, that God has revealed Himself in creation and conscience so that all are accountable, and that God is just and will judge righteously. It does not tell us exactly how God will treat those who have never heard, but it commands the church to take the gospel to all peoples, and assures us that God desires all to be saved.",
    scriptures: ["Romans 1:16-32", "Romans 10:9-17", "Acts 4:12", "Acts 14:15-17", "Acts 17:22-31", "John 14:6", "Genesis 18:25", "Psalms 19:1-6", "1 Timothy 2:3-6", "Matthew 28:18-20"],
    readings: ["John Piper, Let the Nations Be Glad!", "Daniel Strange, The Possibility of Salvation Among the Unevangelised", "Christopher J. H. Wright, The Mission of God", "Four Views on Salvation in a Pluralistic World (Zondervan)"],
    exegetical: `## What God has made known to everyone
"For the invisible things of him from the creation of the world are clearly seen, being understood by the things that are made, even his eternal power and Godhead; so that they are without excuse" (Romans 1:20, KJV). The psalmist says, "The heavens declare the glory of God" (Psalm 19:1). Paul says the Gentiles "shew the work of the law written in their hearts, their conscience also bearing witness" (Romans 2:15, KJV). Everyone knows something of God and of right and wrong.

## What that knowledge does and does not do
Paul argues in Romans 1–3 that everyone has failed to respond properly: "For all have sinned, and come short of the glory of God" (Romans 3:23, KJV). The general revelation leaves people without excuse; it does not by itself save. Salvation comes through the gospel: "For whosoever shall call upon the name of the Lord shall be saved. How then shall they call on him in whom they have not believed? and how shall they believe in him of whom they have not heard?" (Romans 10:13–14, KJV).

## Christ alone
"Neither is there salvation in any other: for there is none other name under heaven given among men, whereby we must be saved" (Acts 4:12, KJV). "I am the way, the truth, and the life: no man cometh unto the Father, but by me" (John 14:6, KJV). The exclusivity of Christ is a consistent apostolic claim.

## Examples in Scripture
- **Melchizedek** (Genesis 14), **Job** and **Jethro** appear as worshippers of the true God outside the line of Abraham.
- **Rahab, Ruth and Naaman** came to know the LORD.
- **Cornelius** (Acts 10) feared God and prayed and gave alms, yet needed to hear the gospel; God sent Peter to him (Acts 10:34–35; 11:14, "Who shall tell thee words, whereby thou and all thy house shall be saved").
- **The Athenians** (Acts 17:22–31) were told that God overlooked times of ignorance but now commands all to repent.
- **Lystra** (Acts 14:15–17): God "left not himself without witness".
The pattern: God sends the gospel to those who respond to what light they have.`,
    archaeological: `- **Evidence of widespread religious longing.** Altars "To the unknown god" appear in Athens (Acts 17:23; Pausanias and Diogenes Laertius mention altars to unknown gods in Athens, though no inscription has been recovered that exactly matches the Acts wording). Paul uses a pagan altar and poets (Epimenides and Aratus, Acts 17:28) as a bridge to the gospel.
- **Monotheistic tendencies.** Some peoples in the ancient world (for example the Egyptian Akhenaten's cult of the Aten, or high-god figures in African traditional religions) held notions of a supreme god. Missionaries have used such notions (like Don Richardson's well-known accounts of "redemptive analogies") to explain the gospel, though one must avoid exaggeration.
- **Spread of the gospel.** Excavations in many regions (Nubia, Ethiopia, Syria, India, Rome, North Africa) show that Christianity spread rapidly in the first centuries by travelling believers.`,
    apostolic: `The apostles responded by going: Paul says, "Woe is unto me, if I preach not the gospel!" (1 Corinthians 9:16, KJV). He aims to preach "where Christ was not named" (Romans 15:20). The apostolic mission shows that the church cannot leave the unreached to speculation.`,
    prophetic: `The prophets promise that the nations will be blessed through Abraham's seed (Genesis 12:3), that God's salvation will reach "the ends of the earth" (Isaiah 49:6), and that "the earth shall be full of the knowledge of the LORD, as the waters cover the sea" (Isaiah 11:9, KJV). Revelation 7:9 sees people from "all nations, and kindreds, and people, and tongues" before the throne.`,
    theological: `## Three broad positions among Christians
1. **Restrictivism (exclusivism):** salvation is only through conscious faith in Christ; those who die without hearing are lost, though justly so; the church must evangelise urgently. Held by most missionary movements.
2. **Inclusivism:** salvation is only through Christ but God may apply His work to some who have not heard but who respond in faith to the light they have (examples such as Abel, Job and Cornelius are used). Held by C. S. Lewis and some evangelicals.
3. **Universalism and pluralism:** all or many religions lead to God; most Christians reject this as contrary to Scripture.

## What we can say safely
- God is just and will judge righteously (Genesis 18:25).
- No one will be condemned for not hearing a message they could not hear; they are judged for how they responded to the light they had.
- No one is saved apart from Christ's work.
- We are not told exactly how God will deal with the unevangelised, and we must not speculate beyond Scripture.
- The church is commanded to preach the gospel to every creature.

## Motivation for missions
This question is not an excuse for complacency: it is the reason for urgency and compassion.`,
    views: `Evangelicals are divided between restrictivist and inclusivist positions; both affirm the necessity of Christ and the duty to evangelise. Roman Catholic teaching (Vatican II) allows that those who through no fault of their own do not know Christ may be saved if they seek God sincerely. Orthodox Christians emphasise God's mercy.`,
    pastoral: `Avoid easy answers. If a friend is asking about a specific loved one, acknowledge the pain and the limits of our knowledge, and point to God's justice and mercy. Encourage the questioner to consider what they themselves have heard: "How shall they hear without a preacher?" Could God be calling them to go or send?` },
];
